    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Max Muncy** (69.72) anchoring the lineup, representing our strongest play. **Max Muncy** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost against a favorable 5.54 xERA matchup, with wind blowing out providing the necessary volatility to elevate his ceiling well above the 60-point threshold. Meanwhile, **James Wood** (62.72) remains a high-leverage start; despite facing a tougher arm in Bradish, his immense park factor boost (+16%) and lead-off spot solidify his role as a primary engine for our category targets, particularly in stolen bases given his athletic profile. \n\nWe are leaning heavily into platoon advantages to navigate the middle of the order. **Brice Turang** (60.08) and **Austin Riley** (60.40) provide reliable output, with **Austin Riley** specifically benefiting from a wind-assisted park factor and a favorable matchup against Ray. The decision to prioritize **Max Muncy** and **Austin Riley** forced a tough call on **Alex Freeland**, who stands as our most significant bench casualty with a 48.73 score. **Alex Freeland** was nearly deployed to bolster our middle infield depth, but the optimizer ultimately favored the superior SP matchup and favorable wind conditions utilized by the active starters. \n\nRisks remain, particularly regarding our outfield efficiency. **Seiya Suzuki** (48.79) and **Jose Caballero** (45.81) occupy the lower end of our starting confidence interval. While **Seiya Suzuki** is in a decent spot due to the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery\u2014offering potential for opportunistic bags\u2014**Jose Caballero** faces a steep 13% penalty due to platoon disadvantage, making him a candidate for mid-game substitution if the game script turns negative early. We are also noting the total absence of **Mickey Moniak** (0.00) from today's active lineup, ensuring we preserve our limited game caps by benching him entirely in favor of higher-floor contributors.",
